Tokenization
What is Tokenization?

Tokenization is the process of breaking text into smaller units called tokens so that a language model can process text as numbers.

For example:

"I love LLMs"

can be split into tokens such as:

["I", "love", "LLMs"]

The exact tokens depend on the tokenizer being used.

Why do LLMs need Tokenization?

Neural networks cannot directly understand raw text.

The text is converted into tokens, and each token is mapped to a numerical token ID.



These token IDs are then used to obtain embeddings that the model can process.

Important Point

A token is not necessarily a whole word.

Depending on the tokenizer, a word can be:

one token
multiple tokens
part of a larger token

For example:

"unhappiness"

might be split into smaller pieces such as:

["un", "happiness"]

This allows LLMs to handle words they may not have seen before.
